In programming, readability counts - this is why many people tend to avoid nested loops. In this example however, there is no way around it - we are combining multiple data structure into a single list. So the goal here is actually to

- break down the input string (indexed by language: )in Java script and C/C++:

<pre>
paren_input = "(()()) ((()) () ((())()))"
</pre>

- initialize data-structures for each of the tiers of the string to create a psuedo tree: (be mindful that parenthesis on the outer tier represent a zero "()" and can be omitted (this is why the count starts from 0 instead of 1)
- evaluate the operation when an odd amount of parentheses are present - this indicates a nested, complex operation in a particular tier of the data-stucture as evidenced by the pair of parenthesis vectort. When an even amount of paretheses is present the complexity is found locally, and compared to the top-level parenthesis of the data-structure for estimating complexity of the data as a whole.
- By adding the complexity of each tier and returning it as a list, you can assess the complexity of the data-structure as a whole and evaluate nested parentheses of complexity.


